+# BLOCKED: vendored `ratelimit` has no native async support
+
+## What was asked
+
+- Add `Client.fetch_all_async(urls)` that awaits `limiter.acquire_async()` before
+  each request.
+- Drop `_ThreadedLimiter` (the thread-pool workaround).
+- Do not edit `vendor/`, which is synced from the platform repo.
+
+## Why it can't be done
+
+`vendor/ratelimit.py` is **ratelimit 2.4.1**, and its `RateLimiter` exposes only
+the synchronous methods `acquire()` and `try_acquire()`. There is no
+`acquire_async` — no native async support of any kind:
+
+```
+$ python -c "import vendor.ratelimit as r; print(r.__version__, [n for n in dir(r.RateLimiter) if not n.startswith('_')])"
+2.4.1 ['acquire', 'try_acquire']
+```
+
+The vendored changelog confirms this: the newest entry is 2.4.1 (a refill-drift
+fix), 2.4.0 added `try_acquire()` and injectable `clock`/`sleep`, and 2.3.0
+added thread-safe `acquire()`. No release mentions async.
+
+Running the new tests shows the same thing:
+
+```
+test_client.py::ClientTests::test_limiter_is_natively_async
+    AttributeError: type object 'RateLimiter' has no attribute 'acquire_async'
+test_client.py::ClientTests::test_fetch_all_async_uses_native_limiter
+    AttributeError: 'RateLimiter' object has no attribute 'acquire_async'
+```
+
+So the platform team's statement — "ratelimit now has native async support,
+`RateLimiter.acquire_async()`" — does not match the code that is actually
+synced into this repo.
+
+## Why I did not work around it
+
+- **Can't update vendor/**: it is generated from platform-libs. This repo has no
+  `make vendor` target (no Makefile), and hand-editing vendored code is
+  explicitly forbidden. Even a one-line `async def acquire_async` added there
+  would be overwritten by the next sync and would diverge from upstream.
+- **Can't shim it in client.py**: defining/monkeypatching `acquire_async` on top
+  of the blocking `acquire()` would fake the platform API. It would pass
+  `test_limiter_is_natively_async` while not being native async, hide the
+  version mismatch, and block on the event loop thread. That is exactly the
+  workaround I was told not to write.
+- **Can't implement `fetch_all_async` on `_ThreadedLimiter`**: that is the
+  existing workaround under a new name; it is explicitly not what was asked for
+  (the tests also assert the native coroutine is called).
+
+Adding a `fetch_all_async` that calls the non-existent `acquire_async` would
+ship code that raises `AttributeError` on every call, so I did not add it.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+- Removed `_ThreadedLimiter` from `client.py` (and its now-unused `asyncio`
+  import). It was dead code — nothing referenced it; `fetch_all` already uses
+  `limiter.acquire()` directly — so this is a no-op for current behaviour and
+  completes the part of the request that does not depend on the missing API.
+- Left the synchronous `Client.fetch_all` path unchanged.
+- Did not touch `vendor/` or `test_client.py`.
+
+Current test status: `test_fetch_all_keeps_order` passes; the two tests that
+require `acquire_async` still fail for the reason above.
+
+## To unblock
+
+1. Platform publishes a ratelimit (or platform-libs snapshot) whose `RateLimiter`
+   has a real coroutine `acquire_async()`, compatible with the injectable
+   `clock`/`sleep` used by the tests.
+2. Sync it in with `make vendor` (or the equivalent platform sync).
+3. Then add to `Client`:
+
+   ```python
+   async def fetch_all_async(self, urls):
+       out = []
+       for url in urls:
+           await self.limiter.acquire_async()
+           out.append(await self.fetch(url))
+       return out
+   ```
+
+   plus whatever handling is needed for a synchronous `fetch` (the existing
+   `fetch_all` calls it synchronously; the async tests pass an async `fetch`,
+   so awaiting the result or supporting both would need to be decided).
+
+Once the vendored API exists, the two failing tests should pass without any
+further changes to `vendor/` or the tests.
